<BaseLayout
  title="Where UniDPP is deep"
  description="The depth of the implementation: verdicts that state what they could not see, byte-identical proofs across languages, and the mechanisms no other passport system offers."
  eyebrow="Reference: depth"
>
  <h1>Where UniDPP is deep</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The capability map states what the framework does. This page states where the
    implementation is deep in handling, and which mechanisms are, as far as the
    passport landscape offers, particular to it. Every claim below names the machinery
    that carries it, and the machinery is public and testable in the open repositories.
  </p>

  <section class="plate" aria-labelledby="honesty-h">
    <h2 id="honesty-h">The honesty machinery</h2>
    <p>
      A verification in this framework answers three readings and, more importantly,
      states what it could not assess. Every answer carries its as-of moment; every
      data element declares a freshness window; an element past its window degrades the
      verdict visibly instead of passing silently; and a claim that outruns its
      evidence appears as reduced coverage rather than as a pass. The conformance
      suite proves the refusals as well as the paths: negative fixtures are part of the
      published test material, because a system that only demonstrates agreement has
      demonstrated little.
    </p>
    <p>
      The canonical bytes of the format are pinned by a shared vector corpus, and the
      corpus reproduces byte-identically across independent implementations in Rust,
      TypeScript, Ruby and Python. The strongest proof is the foreign harness: a
      pure-Python, standard-library-only second implementation that verifies the same
      vectors, signs with the same algorithm, and derives the same outcomes, which is
      how the format demonstrates that it does not secretly depend on one codebase.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="plate" aria-labelledby="unique-h">
    <h2 id="unique-h">Mechanisms particular to the framework</h2>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li>
        <strong>Sovereign suites on one carrier.</strong> A single offline pack carries
        co-signatures from national cryptographic suites, among them the Chinese GM/T
        suite, beside Ed25519 and ECDSA, with post-quantum suites framed and verifiable
        where a deployment enables them. A verifier that cannot compute a suite says so
        and degrades, rather than failing or pretending.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Correlate, never consolidate.</strong> One physical thing may carry
        several codes; the record states that the codes belong to the same subject
        without re-identifying it, because consolidation destroys the schemes the codes
        belong to.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Willingness is law.</strong> A scheme that declines interoperability is
        never brokered around; the refusal names both endpoints and the gap. Where
        interop happens, the receiving profile's acceptance policy decides, and every
        verdict names the policy that judged it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Dark identities.</strong> A passport whose existence is not published
        is not discoverable by guessing, and the refusal is indistinguishable from the
        absence of any record, because enumeration is a privacy attack, not a query.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Mass balance and taint.</strong> Every split and combine carries its
        quantities forward in balance, and a taint on an input reaches its derivatives
        in proportion to what each took, so a recall crosses a supply graph without
        anyone editing a record.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The stateless relay that names no master.</strong> The translation hub
        retains nothing, signs what it forwards, and refuses pairs divided by
        unwillingness; two schemes that speak directly have no need of it, and the
        protocol never suggests otherwise.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The record outlives the issuer.</strong> The notarized archive tier
        re-serves a passport state byte-identically decades later, with archival
        packages drilled as an operational routine.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Machines as principals.</strong> Devices hold manufacture-bound keys
        and commit to their state before they reveal it, so a product's own telemetry
        enters the record under signatures that cannot be retroactively contradicted.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Measurement discipline.</strong> A measured quantity enters with its
        method citation, its calibration chain and its uncertainty, and conformity
        decisions near a limit apply stated decision rules with guard bands, because a
        measurement without those four is a claim.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Federation as a starter kit.</strong> A national registry is packaged
        as an adoptable deployment with its own jurisdiction, identifiers and keys,
        joining the federation through identity-checked discovery rather than through
        a permission.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="plate" aria-labelledby="extensible-h">
    <h2 id="extensible-h">Extensible in scope</h2>
    <p>
      The framework is international by construction and extensible by data. A new
      jurisdiction mounts as a dated profile binding on the one core; a new protocol is
      a render profile, so presenting the same record in another scheme's shape is a
      rendering and never a fork; units, vocabularies and code lists are register items
      with supersession; and cross-scheme translation goes through a mapping discipline
      that states its losses rather than hiding them.
    </p>
    <p>
      The same openness extends to kinds of passport. A class of passport is a
      registered, signed profile of data elements, units and views, and an instance
      states which features it carries and how deep each goes. The honest boundary
      rides with the honesty machinery: authoring a new class today is expert work
      against the published schema, and the guided designer for it is declared work
      ahead; the questions this raises are answered directly in the
      <a href="https://docs.unidpp.org/get-started/faq/">frequently asked questions</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

